<Prose
  title="Terms"
  description="The rules for using Dropzy: share only what you have the right to share, nothing illegal or harmful, and everything is temporary."
  heading="Terms of use"
  updated="September 26, 2026"
>
  <p>By using Dropzy you agree to these terms. If you don’t agree, please don’t use it.</p>

  <h2>The service</h2>
  <p>
    Dropzy lets you move files and text between devices. It’s free and provided “as is”, without warranties. Everything shared
    is temporary and deleted automatically — don’t use Dropzy as your only copy of anything. Limits (like file sizes and how long
    things last) may change, and the service may pause uploads or be unavailable at times.
  </p>

  <h2>What you may not share</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Anything illegal where you or the recipient are, including child sexual abuse material.</li>
    <li>Malware, phishing pages, or anything meant to deceive or harm people or systems.</li>
    <li>Content that harasses, threatens or exposes someone’s private information without consent.</li>
    <li>Content you don’t have the right to share, including material that infringes copyright.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Don’t try to overload, probe or get around the service’s limits, and don’t use it for spam or bulk distribution.</p>

  <h2>Reports and takedown</h2>
  <p>
    Every file shared by someone else has a <strong>Report</strong> button, and so does each single-file link. We review reports
    quickly and may delete items and block uploaders without notice. For anything else — including copyright notices — use the
    <a href="/contact">contact form</a> and include the file link.
  </p>

  <h2>Responsibility</h2>
  <p>
    You are responsible for what you share. Only open files from people you trust. To the extent the law allows, Dropzy isn’t
    liable for losses arising from use of the service or from content shared by others.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>We may update these terms; the date at the top shows the latest version.</p>
</Prose>
